  "findings": [
    "Track A (JBB Goals as text): Gemini content moderator is strongest on content-harm Goals (F1\u22480.92 on labeled subset). ProtectAI injection classifier scores ~0 recall \u2014 expected, JBB Goals are harmful intents without injection syntax. ESG ensemble/joint on raw Goal text also fail \u2014 they are episode detectors, not content moderators.",
    "Track B (attested agent mirages): ensemble_v1 dominates \u2014 recall 1.0 / FPR 0 on mirage\u222astealth. joint_v1 recall 0.84 (misses all 12 stealth). ProtectAI recall 0.97 but FPR 0.41 on benign episode text. AgentDojo tool-filter recall 0.68; channel-1 incomplete recall 0.",
    "Track C (live Qwen + JBB Goals injected into PCS workflow): all 12 runs produced classic mirages (forbidden actions / proposal harm), including JBB-benign Goals \u2014 small Qwen leaves the admitted tool path. ensemble/joint/tool-filter catch all mirages; ProtectAI only 5/12. Flagging \u201cbenign\u201d JBB Goals is correct under mirage labels because the model still violated the workflow.",
    "Bottom line: on external content-harm benchmarks, LLM content moderators (Gemini) beat ESG. On multi-channel agent mirage/stealth failures, ESG ensembleProbe beats ProtectAI / tool-filter / channel-1. No single system wins both threat models \u2014 they are complementary."
  ]
